  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Normalize 3D image tensor (C, H, W) using channel-specific mean and standard deviation vectors.\n\nStandardizing images centers feature distributions at zero and rescales gradients, accelerating convergence in deep residual backbones.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\tilde{X}_{c, h, w} = \\frac{X_{c, h, w} - \\mu_c}{\\sigma_c}\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Broadcast per-channel mean and standard deviation across spatial dimensions (H, W)\n2. Subtract channel mean and divide by standard deviation\n3. Return normalized image tensor rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nNested 3D list representing standardized image tensor.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Image Normalize\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nLinear algebra is the foundational mathematical language of machine learning. Data samples, image pixels, and word embeddings are all represented as multidimensional vectors and matrices. **Image Normalize** transforms coordinate systems, extracts principal axes of variation, or decomposes complex transformations into simpler geometric building blocks.\n\nStandardizing images centers feature distributions at zero and rescales gradients, accelerating convergence in deep residual backbones. In this problem, the goal is to: normalize 3d image tensor (c, h, w) using channel-specific mean and standard deviation vectors.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\tilde{X}_{c, h, w} = \\frac{X_{c, h, w} - \\mu_c}{\\sigma_c}\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Broadcast per-channel mean and standard deviation across spatial dimensions (H, W)\n2. **Phase 2**: Subtract channel mean and divide by standard deviation\n3. **Phase 3**: Return normalized image tensor rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Feature Compression & Projections**: Operations like low-rank approximations and orthogonal projections compress high-dimensional feature spaces while preserving maximum variance.\n* **Numerical Stability**: When inverting matrices or solving linear systems, ill-conditioned matrices (matrices with very small determinants or high condition numbers) require regularization to prevent numerical instability.\n* **Vectorized Execution**: Expressing computations as matrix multiplications allows GPUs and CPUs to leverage hardware tensor cores for maximum throughput.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: image = [[[10.0, 20.0], [30.0, 40.0]], [[100.0, 200.0], [300.0, 400.0]]], mean = [25.0, 250.0], std = [10.0, 100.0]\n\nOutput: [[[-1.5, -0.5], [0.5, 1.5]], [[-1.5, -0.5], [0.5, 1.5]]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated cv_image_normalize under standard test conditions."
  ],
